NOTE ON LONG-ARM STATUTES
Our cases so far in this chapter and our next two cases, World-Wide Volkswagen and J. McIntyre Machinery, consider the constraints that the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment place on state courts’ exercise of personal jurisdiction. In addition to the U.S. Constitution, state law may place limits on state courts’ exercise of personal jurisdiction.

Hess and International Shoe provide examples of early-twentieth-century state statutes enabling state courts to exercise personal jurisdiction in specific circumstances. After International Shoe announced a new, more permissive paradigm for personal jurisdiction, states greatly expanded the scope of state laws governing their courts’ power to exercise personal jurisdiction. These laws today are known as “long-arm statutes.”

A long-arm statute is a statute defining the scope of a state court’s jurisdiction broadly, such that it reaches (with its “long arm”) defendants far from
