the state. Typically, courts interpret such statutes to authorize state courts to exercise personal jurisdiction to the maximum extent permitted by the United States Constitution. Sometimes, the statute is not explicit in this regard. The language may simply be broad enough that the state court interprets the statute to stretch jurisdiction as far as the federal constitution will tolerate. States are free to define by statute or state constitution their courts’ jurisdiction more narrowly than the Fourteenth Amendment’s Due Process Clause allows.

In principle, determining the validity of a state court’s exercise of personal jurisdiction is a two-step inquiry: First, does state law authorize the exercise of jurisdiction over the defendant? Second, is this exercise of jurisdiction consistent with federal due process? An important consequence of long-arm statutes is that for most state courts, these two steps collapse into a single step: Is the exercise of personal jurisdiction consistent with the U.S. Constitution? An example of this is our next case, World-Wide Volkswagen.
